# Who gets asked to review, without anybody having to remember to ask.
#
# main's protection requires one approving review, so every pull request needs
# a reviewer named on it. Adding that reviewer by hand is a step that is easy
# to forget and adds nothing - the answer is the same every time, because this
# repository has one human. GitHub requests a review from a code owner
# automatically when a pull request touches a path they own, so declaring it
# once here is the whole job.
#
# It applies to pull requests this repository's automation opens as well -
# Claude's, Dependabot's - which is the case that actually matters, since
# those are the ones nobody is sitting in front of at the time.
#
# One catch worth knowing: GitHub never requests a review from the author of a
# pull request. A pull request jlemberg opens gets no request from this file,
# which is correct - it would be asking someone to approve their own work -
# but it does mean that one still needs a reviewer chosen by hand, or the
# admin bypass.
#
# A second catch, this one silent: an unmatched or misspelled owner is not an
# error. GitHub simply requests nothing and reports the problem only in a UI
# panel nobody opens. `gh api repos/fensberg/homelab/codeowners/errors` is the
# way to ask directly, and is worth running after editing this file.
#
# This does NOT make code-owner review *required* - that is the separate
# "Require review from Code Owners" toggle in main's branch protection, which
# is an admin setting rather than a file. Without it, the review is requested
# but any approving review satisfies the rule. With one human either way, the
# distinction is currently academic; it stops being academic the day a second
# maintainer exists.

